Hello,
Please consider providing a capability to extend end-application's startup in the following way: 

Startup.ThirdPartyLib.cs
```
using System;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Extensions;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

[assembly: HostingStartup(typeof(Blazor.ServerSide.ThirdPartyLibHostingStartup))]

namespace Blazor.ServerSide {
    public partial class ThirdPartyLibStartupFilter : IStartupFilter {

        public ThirdPartyLibStartupFilter(ILoggerFactory loggerFactory) {
            //
        }

        public Action<IApplicationBuilder> Configure(Action<IApplicationBuilder> next) {
            return builder => {
                builder.UseMiddleware<...>();
                next(builder);
            };
        }
    }
    public partial class ThirdPartyLibHostingStartup : IHostingStartup {
        void IHostingStartup.Configure(IWebHostBuilder builder) {
            builder.ConfigureServices((serviceCollection) => {
                serviceCollection.AddTransient<IStartupFilter, ThirdPartyLibStartupFilter>();
                serviceCollection.AddScoped<...>();
            });
        }
    }
}

```

Use case:
Nuget package copies own startup template file ( Startup.%ThirdPartyLibName%.cs ) into end application after installation via msbuild target to simplify a first time setup for a new user. The described approach works fine for serverside blazor since it's based on top of aspcore, but it has a lack of functionality in case wasm

Altogether this looks great to me.

> This is the code that we expect to appear in every template.

This is not a big deal, and I know we tend to prefer not adding explanatory comments to templates, but do you think the unfamiliarity of this new arrangement might warrant giving an extra clue about where to configure services now there's no `ConfigureServices` method? For example,

```cs
async static Task Main(string[] args)
{
    var builder = WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args);
    builder.RootComponents.Add<App>("app");

    // To configure services in your application, use builder.Services

    await builder.Build().RunAsync();
}
```

> Configuration as part of WebAssemblyHostBuilder

I do think that would be really valuable. Maybe even necessary. That would make it much more convenient if you're trying to do something like:

```cs
builder.Services.AddSingleton(() => new MyGrpcClient(builder.Configuration["BackendUri"]));
```

Otherwise it's very difficult to have DI services that require config info as constructor parameters rather than a subsequent `Init` call.

I know we haven't exactly nailed down the mechanics of how the config info gets into the system yet.

> Should we rename one of the two .Services properties for clarity

If we're doing this, then I think the simpler name (`Services`) should be reserved for `builder.Services`, since that's by far the more common usage. Then maybe we'd have `host.ServiceProvider`. That seems reasonably usable to me.

> What's the cost (in size) of including IConfiguration and IConfigurationBuilder

I'd say we should design whatever we think is the nicest API to use, and then measure the cost. Only if the cost is problematic (say, adds > 10KB) then we can do some gymnastics to find a way to get the linker to be able to trim it out.

> I do think that would be really valuable. Maybe even necessary. That would make it much more convenient if you're trying to do something like:

> builder.Services.AddSingleton(() => new MyGrpcClient(builder.Configuration["BackendUri"]));

I'm afraid it's not that straightforward. It'll end up being this instead:

```C#
builder.Services.AddSingleton(s =>
{
    return new MyGrpcClient(s.GetRequiredService<IConfiguration>()["BackendUri"]);
}
```

The problem is that if `IConfiguration` is *baked* by the time you get to manipulate services, then how do you influence the configuration before it gets *baked*? `WebAssemblyHostBuilder` has an `IConfigurationBuilder `, and `WebAssemblyHost` has `IConfiguration`.

It's not terribly onerous, but it's not as simple as you suggested.

> If we're doing this, then I think the simpler name (Services) should be reserved for builder.Services, since that's by far the more common usage. Then maybe we'd have host.ServiceProvider. That seems reasonably usable to me.

I'd put forward `host.ApplicationServices` instead, that's what we do elsewhere.

